    "hints": [
      "Merging two lists pairwise until one remains works, but early lists get walked over and over.",
      "The next node in the answer is always the smallest front node among all k lists.",
      "Keep the k front nodes in a min-heap: pop the smallest, attach it, push its successor. k exchanges per node total."
    ],
    "solution": "Min-heap of the k front nodes: pop the smallest, attach it to the result, and push its successor. Every node passes through the heap once: O(N log k) time for N total nodes, O(k) heap space.",
    "solutionCode": "import heapq\n\ndef merge_k_lists(lists):\n    heap = [head for head in lists if head is not None]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    head = None\n    tail = None\n    while heap:\n        node = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if head is None:\n            head = tail = node\n        else:\n            tail.next = node\n            tail = node\n        if node.next is not None:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, node.next)\n    return head\n",
    "bruteForceCode": "def merge_k_lists(lists):\n    def merge_two(a, b):\n        head = None\n        tail = None\n        while a is not None and b is not None:\n            if a.value <= b.value:\n                small = a\n                a = a.next\n            else:\n                small = b\n                b = b.next\n            if head is None:\n                head = tail = small\n            else:\n                tail.next = small\n                tail = small\n        rest = a if a is not None else b\n        if rest is not None:\n            if head is None:\n                head = rest\n            else:\n                tail.next = rest\n        return head\n    merged = None\n    for list_head in lists:\n        merged = merge_two(merged, list_head)\n    return merged\n",
