    "topics": [
      "backtracking"
    ],
    "source": "canonical N-Queens concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
    "description": "Place n queens on an n by n board so that no two share a row, column, or diagonal; exactly one queen sits in each row. Return every distinct solution as a list of boards, where each board is a list of n strings made of \"Q\" and \".\" characters. Sort the list of boards ascending so the output is deterministic.",
    "constraints": [
      "4 <= n <= 8"
    ],
    "parameters": [
      "n"
    ],
    "functionName": "solve_n_queens",
    "starterCode": "def solve_n_queens(n):\n    # Return all solutions as sorted boards of \"Q\"/\".\" strings.\n    ...\n",
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": {
          "n": 4
        },
        "output": [
          [
            "..Q.",
            "Q...",
            "...Q",
            ".Q.."
          ],
          [
            ".Q..",
            "...Q",
            "Q...",
            "..Q."
          ]
        ],
        "explanation": "The 4 by 4 board has exactly two placements."
      },
      {
        "input": {
          "n": 6
        },
        "output": [
          [
            "....Q.",
            "..Q...",
            "Q.....",
            ".....Q",
            "...Q..",
            ".Q...."
          ],
          [
            "...Q..",
            "Q.....",
            "....Q.",
            ".Q....",
            ".....Q",
            "..Q..."
          ],
          [
            "..Q...",
            ".....Q",
            ".Q....",
            "....Q.",
            "Q.....",
            "...Q.."
          ],
          [
            ".Q....",
            "...Q..",
            ".....Q",
            "Q.....",
            "..Q...",
            "....Q."
          ]
        ],
        "explanation": "The 6 by 6 board has four placements."
      }
    ],
    "hints": [
      "One queen per row is forced, so the search is over columns: pick a column for row 0, then row 1, and so on.",
      "Columns and both diagonal directions need their own occupancy marks; row minus col and row plus col each identify a diagonal.",
      "Backtrack: mark, recurse into the next row, unmark. Render a board whenever the last row is placed, then sort at the end."
    ],
    "solution": "Backtracking row by row with column, diagonal, and anti-diagonal occupancy marks: each placement check is O(1), and boards render when all rows are placed. O(n!) search nodes with O(n) work each; output sorted for determinism.",
    "solutionCode": "def solve_n_queens(n):\n    solutions = []\n    columns = [False] * n\n    diagonals = [False] * (2 * n)\n    anti = [False] * (2 * n)\n    rows = []\n    def place(row):\n        if row == n:\n            solutions.append([\".\" * col + \"Q\" + \".\" * (n - col - 1) for col in rows])\n            return\n        for col in range(n):\n            d = row - col + n\n            a = row + col\n            if columns[col] or diagonals[d] or anti[a]:\n                continue\n            columns[col] = diagonals[d] = anti[a] = True\n            rows.append(col)\n            place(row + 1)\n            rows.pop()\n            columns[col] = diagonals[d] = anti[a] = False\n    place(0)\n    return sorted(solutions)\n",
    "bruteForceCode": "def solve_n_queens(n):\n    solutions = []\n    rows = []\n    def conflicts(row, col):\n        for other_row, other_col in enumerate(rows):\n            if other_col == col or abs(other_row - row) == abs(other_col - col):\n                return True\n        return False\n    def place(row):\n        if row == n:\n            solutions.append([\".\" * c + \"Q\" + \".\" * (n - c - 1) for c in rows])\n            return\n        for col in range(n):\n            if not conflicts(row, col):\n                rows.append(col)\n                place(row + 1)\n                rows.pop()\n    place(0)\n    return sorted(solutions)\n",
    "failureHint": "hidden tests include the board sizes five, seven, and eight",
    "expectedComplexity": {
      "time": "O(n!)",
      "space": "O(n)"
    },
